WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.760
A little while back I saw a reddit post and linked&nbsp;
article, both written by Joshua Wood, and it went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.040
something like this—Brackets are a useful tool,&nbsp;
but they have limitations. They do cover power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.880
level to some extent, but they don’t address&nbsp;
specific play pattern preferences aside from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.920
following general community preferences such as&nbsp;
limitations on mana denial in lower brackets. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.200
good solution to this problem is to have&nbsp;
players come to the table with a basic 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.000
second pitch for what their deck is doing. An&nbsp;
example of this might be “This is my Gonti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.520
Canny Acquisitor rogue deck. It’s a theft&nbsp;
deck that wants to hit people with evasive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.400
Rogues to steal cards, and eventually pump&nbsp;
up my rogues for the win. It’s bracket 3.”

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.360
First thoughts, this is fine, a very normal&nbsp;
opinion to have and a reasonable suggestion. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.320
already do some version of this, as do most people&nbsp;
I play with in public. At least half the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.920
when I’m reading the commander of a stranger I’m&nbsp;
playing against, or when I make a comment about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.800
it, they’ll drop me a basic pitch for how they’re&nbsp;
using the commander and what their deck is doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.000
The only critique I might have of this&nbsp;
idea is that it’s maybe a little odd to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.320
have a pitch like this rehearsed word for word and&nbsp;
delivered like some sort of theatrical monologue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.680
but the broader point about communication&nbsp;
is good—or so I thought. This post was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.520
all that popular on r/EDH—63 upvotes&nbsp;
and 89 comments at time of recording,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.840
with some comments critical of the idea&nbsp;
getting more upvotes than the post itself.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.360
[Arcosa] Revealing what your deck does by&nbsp;
playing it sounds way more fun to me than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.360
everyone giving a synopsis on how the&nbsp;
game is gonna go before it even starts.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.520
[Quips] You don't need to tell other&nbsp;
players your deck’s life story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.400
an interesting fact, or even a basic&nbsp;
strategy. Once everyone chooses their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.040
commanders and commanders are revealed,&nbsp;
they can deduce strategies from there.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.880
[Rebell] Leaving some mystery to the&nbsp;
game is fun. It means we can play to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.240
figure out each other's weakness&nbsp;
instead of basically being told.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.920
[Snail] In the broader scheme of things,&nbsp;
this doesn’t matter. A modestly visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.400
reddit post attracted some people with opinions I&nbsp;
disagree with who then got a handful of upvotes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.320
big whoop. However, I can’t get this&nbsp;
line of thinking out of my brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.440
and these opinions are similar to comments I’ve&nbsp;
received on my channel. There seems to be a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.600
online faction of commander players who think&nbsp;
this way, who treat commander as some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.520
detective game where a large part of the gameplay&nbsp;
is figuring out what’s going on in opposing decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.680
or alternatively as some sort of show that&nbsp;
you wouldn’t want to spoil the ending of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.520
So, I’m going to take this post as a jumping&nbsp;
off point to talk about that broad faction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.920
this group of players that I find fascinating,&nbsp;
and I’m going to see if I can figure out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.240
motivates their thinking, why they’re like this,&nbsp;
and if there’s anything to the vision of commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.200
they strive after. As a disclaimer—this video is&nbsp;
more conceptual and social-theory-oriented than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.760
most of what I make, so I’d recommend treating&nbsp;
it as some fun ideas to chew on rather than cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.240
hard facts to destroy the libs with or whatever.&nbsp;
Though this is a sort of a soft response to The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.600
Trinket Mage’s video about me in April, so I guess&nbsp;
this can be cold hard facts to destroy him with.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.760
[Trinket narrating]

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.440
EDH is typically a casual format, which simply&nbsp;
means that players aren’t necessarily expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.360
to try as hard as they can to win, either in-game&nbsp;
or in deckbuilding. This is challenging for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.520
because, in any sort of game setting, a lot of&nbsp;
people will instinctually look for some sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.480
of goal to optimize toward. We can see this&nbsp;
play out in something like D&amp;D—role-playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.040
games are even more casual than casual EDH&nbsp;
in a lot of ways, but many players will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.240
still put a lot of effort into making their&nbsp;
characters as powerful as possible in combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.120
This is kind of pointless on a party level since&nbsp;
the DM can literally make combats easier or harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.800
on a whim to suit the players, and even harder in&nbsp;
a way that’s targeted to a specific player. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.760
the power level of a character remains a thing&nbsp;
that people optimize because they need a goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.320
while building out a character sheet—something&nbsp;
needs to motivate the decisions being made. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.360
same goes for EDH—people will create goals in the&nbsp;
deckbuilding process, and one of those goals will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.560
frequently be making a deck that is good under&nbsp;
some set of criteria, at least if you’re not one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.640
of the mythical bracket 1 gamers. So that will&nbsp;
be a part of deckbuilding, but what about in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.240
games? There are all sorts of skills tested in a&nbsp;
competitive 1v1 magic environment—turn sequencing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.400
mastery of a given decklist, assessment of&nbsp;
opposing threats, probabilistic thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.080
playing to outs, and so on. A 1v1 Magic tournament&nbsp;
will stress-test a player’s skills in all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.200
things to the limit, and in many cases games will&nbsp;
be won and lost by the collective weight of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:29.440
lot of tiny decisions. As in deckbuilding, EDH’s&nbsp;
casual nature makes things trickier in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.120
that winning isn’t as dominant a goal, and plays&nbsp;
that are suboptimal in the strict sense might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.600
necessarily be considered misplays in the same&nbsp;
way they are in 60 card formats. As a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.080
of this fact, a lot of EDH players subconsciously&nbsp;
go looking for something to replace some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:51.200
competitive skills, and in a format as diverse&nbsp;
as EDH, a very common substitute is knowledge.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.040
A certain subset of EDH players seem almost&nbsp;
obsessed with knowing as many possible plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.000
their opponents can do as possible. This is&nbsp;
especially true for players playing at a level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.520
with sudden ways to win, where not being able to&nbsp;
recognize a combo piece or central enabler for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.560
opponent’s deck can rapidly lead to a game loss.&nbsp;
Whether it’s as an outlet for an underutilized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.600
competitive instinct or as a trauma response to&nbsp;
getting OTK’d one too many times, being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.240
shrewdly navigate the ins and outs of opposing&nbsp;
plays and figure out how threatening a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:27.280
card is within the entire landscape of the EDH&nbsp;
format is a skill that a lot of players have built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:32.640
up. This is especially true for commanders—a lot&nbsp;
of commanders have a handful of cards they pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.200
pretty powerfully with, and having it locked away&nbsp;
in your dome what those cards might be for a given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.280
commander can give you a leg up in determining&nbsp;
the possibilities for what an opponent might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.320
doing. And, if we assume that most decks with a&nbsp;
given commander will be somewhat similar, having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.040
an encyclopedic knowledge of commanders and what&nbsp;
their typical decks look like can actually serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.920
as somewhat of a replacement for rule 0. I don’t&nbsp;
even need to have a conversation with my opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.120
all I need to do is see that Teysa pull up and I’m&nbsp;
already squaring up for some Ashnod’s Altar BS.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.360
So this is a skill that a lot of players have&nbsp;
put effort into honing, whether consciously or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.800
unconsciously, and it shouldn’t be surprising&nbsp;
that a lot of them want to use this skill. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.040
for a player who has spent a lot of time around&nbsp;
various magic cards and is fully engaged in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.000
game, it’s not hard to imagine this being a lot of&nbsp;
fun. I see my opponent play a Blood Artist effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.920
and I start to look for possible loops, think&nbsp;
about what sac outlets and supporting pieces are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.240
in-color for my opponent. I see a Karn’s Bastion&nbsp;
hit the board, and start trying to deduce what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.640
counters are soon to pop into existence. I see&nbsp;
an opponent with two lands untapped on my turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.240
and I try to figure out what sorts of disruption&nbsp;
they might be able to put together with red, blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.160
and white mana. I would be lying if I claimed&nbsp;
that the description of the commander format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.960
I just described doesn’t sound fun—it’s&nbsp;
appealing to play a game like that. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:49.840
what’s the problem? Surely there’s nothing&nbsp;
wrong with people enjoying the format this way.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.600
[Elk narrating]

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.840
Comedic name-calling aside, this is my most&nbsp;
basic issue with the vision of the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.400
format I just presented. I dedicated a full&nbsp;
20 minute video to this topic last summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:10.160
but here’s the short version. The EDH format has&nbsp;
become effectively the onboarding format of paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.800
Magic, the way new players get into the game and&nbsp;
the way a lot of them continue to play the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.600
This is cool in a lot of ways, and there’s one&nbsp;
especially new-player-friendly element at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.080
heart of the commander format—it doesn’t have a&nbsp;
meaningful metagame. You don’t have to learn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.760
opposing decks you might be facing and figure&nbsp;
out what decks can fare well against those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.600
and more importantly you won’t have to worry&nbsp;
that your deck will be too weak to face the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.680
competition. This is wonderful for creativity in&nbsp;
deckbuilding—60 card magic typically relegates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:42.080
“creativity” or “brewing” to players with a&nbsp;
relatively strong grasp of a given format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.880
but since EDH lacks a metagame or set power&nbsp;
level, players of any skill can innovate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:51.840
create something of their own rather than needing&nbsp;
to netdeck. This is an undoubtedly nice feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.240
but on the flip side, this lack of a metagame&nbsp;
ironically also poses an entirely different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:01.520
set of challenges onto players that aren’t&nbsp;
found in a competitive 1v1 format. You can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.760
learn the 4 most common decks you’ll be playing&nbsp;
because there are thousands of possible decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.800
you might find yourself facing. This might be&nbsp;
manageable in a playgroup with unchanging decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:16.320
but playing out in the wild with strangers is&nbsp;
a whole different story. Any set of 4 EDH decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.800
will likely have more than 200 unique nonland&nbsp;
cards, which is a fair amount to learn if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:25.680
decks are unchanging, and an ongoing gauntlet for&nbsp;
newer players in a public setting where each game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.640
might present dozens of new cards. I think most&nbsp;
of us have been in a game among strangers where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.720
it becomes clear that one or more players are&nbsp;
checked out from what opponents are doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.560
fatigued by the process of keeping up with all&nbsp;
the new cardboard that’s hitting the table.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.760
So that’s the case I’m trying to present here.&nbsp;
EDH has 3x the opponents and often around 10x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.720
the number of unique cards in opposing decks&nbsp;
as compared to 1v1 formats. There are 30,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.520
cards legal in the format now, and thousands of&nbsp;
brand new cards entering the format every year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.760
Each deck tends to play far more permanents than&nbsp;
are seen in 1v1 formats due to the higher life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.240
totals and the multiplayer aspect of things,&nbsp;
and the result is that boards rapidly clog up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.280
with assorted permanents for players to keep&nbsp;
track of. The information density of EDH is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.320
untouched by any other widely-played format, and&nbsp;
that is a feature about it that we need to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.360
in mind when we’re playing it. All of this is&nbsp;
why I find the “knowledge competition” version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:24.080
of casual EDH frustrating, and seek to reduce it&nbsp;
as much as possible. And that’s as somebody who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:29.040
is probably better equipped to threat analyze&nbsp;
than at least 80% of EDH players—it’s not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.920
I can’t figure out how potentially game-ending&nbsp;
a sequencing of opposing plays is on the fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:39.040
it’s that I don’t think that should necessarily&nbsp;
be a constant task expected of all EDH players.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.440
The game of figuring out what my opponent’s deck&nbsp;
does is just not one I think is beneficial to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.680
have at the center of the EDH format. There are&nbsp;
even plenty of days that I personally wish to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.920
avoid this part of the format, to kick my feet&nbsp;
back and focus on resolving my cascade triggers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.600
instead of Sherlock-Holmesing my way to what the&nbsp;
white-knuckled powergamer across the table is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.280
packing. But still. With all that said, I could&nbsp;
see somebody thinking that I’m throwing out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:06.080
baby with the bath water here. Sure, knowing a&nbsp;
rough outline of what opposing decks are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.560
might reduce the mental load of the format&nbsp;
and make it more approachable for all people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:16.720
but what about the theatricality of it? What about&nbsp;
surprises and zany moments and unexpected twists?

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:20.800
[BDD Narrating]

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.880
A fairly common mistake that’s made among&nbsp;
newer game masters in a role playing game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.240
like D&amp;D or Pathfinder is placing too much&nbsp;
importance on their own predetermined plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:37.520
and especially trying to create plot moments that&nbsp;
will generate a notable reaction in players. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.000
strange hilltop village? It’s actually&nbsp;
on the back of a massive creature. Whoa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.720
dude! Now you’re telling me these past 5 sessions&nbsp;
have been playing out in the mind of Jackson the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:51.840
Frost Dragon during his fight with this world’s&nbsp;
real enemy, Lord Xyrox? What a revelation!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:57.040
This isn’t to say that creating unexpected plot&nbsp;
beats is never a good idea, but it’s not a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:02.480
thing to dump most of your effort into as a GM or&nbsp;
make a load-bearing centerpiece of your campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.040
because it’s not really what makes a multiplayer&nbsp;
RPG fun. Most players aren’t showing up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.520
sessions to be recited your half-baked book idea,&nbsp;
they’re there for collaborative storytelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.000
for experiencing funky situations outside the&nbsp;
realm of reality, for throwing around die rolls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.320
and getting to play with the fun character they&nbsp;
crafted. It might feel good to imagine your big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.640
reveal getting the same emotional response as&nbsp;
some other big moment that arises organically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:28.960
but that’s not necessarily going to happen.&nbsp;
At this point you’re probably asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:34.640
what does this have to do with EDH, Alex? Well,&nbsp;
a very similar dynamic takes place there. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:39.440
dedicated a whole video to this topic about&nbsp;
a year ago, but the basic idea there was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:45.040
there are some inherent and unavoidable problems&nbsp;
with decks that like to win in some grandiose way.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.520
I have a deck that wins by getting three Fiend&nbsp;
Hunters in play at the same time to fiend-hunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:54.080
each other indefinitely and cobble together an&nbsp;
ETB-based win from there. I also brewed a list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:58.880
with the goal of casting Rograkh an arbitrarily&nbsp;
large number of times before Jeska-ing all three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:04.240
of my opponents for an arbitrarily large amount&nbsp;
of damage. These are undoubtedly flashy as far as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:09.440
wincons go, and they really play into the “showing&nbsp;
off” side of EDH, the part of the format that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:14.320
is driven by the desire to have strangers ooh&nbsp;
and ah at the cool things your deck is doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.600
In principle this is fine, but in reality you tend&nbsp;
to get the desired response a lot less than you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:24.400
expect with this kind of deck. EDH is already&nbsp;
full of combo decks that can pull off cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:28.320
explosive wins, and even if your&nbsp;
combo deck is a cut above the rest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:32.400
it’s still putting together a handful of&nbsp;
cards that abruptly end the game. Effectively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.200
this sort of deck ends up being more fun&nbsp;
to build than to play—it’s fun to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.760
and tune a deck that does something wacky and&nbsp;
unusual, but something being fun to think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.640
fun to theorize, and fun to hear described as&nbsp;
a concept doesn’t necessarily make it fun in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:52.880
actual game. Because that’s what EDH is—a game.&nbsp;
It’s not a museum of cool card interactions, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.800
not a collaborative art installation, it’s not a&nbsp;
competition to see who can do the wackiest thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:02.720
it’s a game with players and pieces and goals.&nbsp;
It’s not identical to a conventional board game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:05.840
obviously—in the best of times I’d argue&nbsp;
it can be much more interesting than your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.000
average medium-complexity board game—but it&nbsp;
should be a lot closer to that than it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.360
reading off a list of the most interesting&nbsp;
things you can do with Second Harvest.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:18.320
So that’s my precautionary note about funky combo&nbsp;
decks, and it also applies to any deck whose main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:22.320
gameplan is a bit of a one-trick pony, such as a&nbsp;
deck that is built to cascade into a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.640
threat every game. Still, I’m guessing that&nbsp;
argument probably isn’t all that persuasive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:31.600
to a lot of people in the context of pregame&nbsp;
conversations. Sure, a deck that plans to win in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:36.800
some sudden, dramatic way shouldn’t be hiding its&nbsp;
gameplan, but what about cool plays that aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.280
game-winning? Are you just asking me to cough up&nbsp;
all my deck’s secrets for my opponents to see, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.720
spill the beans on my decks’ cool intricacies? If&nbsp;
I’m to be totally honest, I don’t quite know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.760
to respond to this sort of question, because to me&nbsp;
it’s a clear indication that the person saying it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:57.600
and myself are really not on the same page about&nbsp;
the terms of the discussion. So, let’s get on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:02.640
same page—what is the purpose of a conversation&nbsp;
at or near the start of a game where players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:08.880
describe their decks? Two things. One, it’s a&nbsp;
rule zero conversation—anytime you’re mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:13.120
the bracket of your deck, how it plans to win,&nbsp;
and what its most powerful plays are, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:17.120
giving your opponents the opportunity to decide&nbsp;
if they want to play against that. This is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:22.320
I find combo decks that win in some cool way the&nbsp;
most relevant example—even if you’d quite like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.960
keep your cool combo a secret to put on some sort&nbsp;
of dramatic performance for anybody watching, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:30.400
you’re playing that sort of deck you really should&nbsp;
be making it clear to your opponents that that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:34.720
what you’re doing. This could be as simple as&nbsp;
saying “I’m playing a deck with a combo win that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:38.880
frequently happens around turn 7,” which gives&nbsp;
your opponents the tools they need to decide if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:44.080
they want to be in this game with a moderate speed&nbsp;
combo deck. In contrast to this, a deck whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:49.760
cool thing isn’t a combo win might not have this&nbsp;
sort of relevant obligation in the rule 0 phase.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:54.160
The other purpose of a start-of-game conversation&nbsp;
is getting players calibrated about what’s to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.160 --> 00:15:00.000
come, and I actually think of this as the more&nbsp;
important purpose. A lot of players will be okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.960
playing against a somewhat spicy deck so long as&nbsp;
they get some idea of what to expect and what to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:09.840
watch out for. With my most powerful deck, my&nbsp;
Francisco Malcolm deck, I’ll generally tell my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:15.440
opponents that my deck is fast, with frequent turn&nbsp;
5 wins, and that it usually wins with Time Sieve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:18.640
and with these two things in their pocket&nbsp;
my opponents will have a decent idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:23.680
what to watch out for and the tools to make&nbsp;
strategic decisions against my deck. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.680
with my Sharuum deck I’ll tell my opponents that&nbsp;
my deck is a control deck built around Astral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:32.480
Slide. This is an exceptionally short description&nbsp;
of my deck that leaves dozens of cool interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:37.040
unmentioned, but it communicates the two most&nbsp;
important facts my opponents might want to know:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:40.480
that my deck will be able to exert more&nbsp;
leverage over the game the longer it goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:45.200
and that flickering permanents is a central&nbsp;
part of my gameplan. Performing this sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:49.440
“calibration” is a good practice because&nbsp;
EDH is fundamentally a social format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:53.440
and one where players should be thinking about&nbsp;
their opponents’ decks and strategizing the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.680
way to navigate around what their opponents are&nbsp;
playing. If players don’t have basic information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:01.680
on their opponents’ decks, they’re in the&nbsp;
dark. Who’s the threat now? Who might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.360
the threat later? What on board currently&nbsp;
is threaten-ing? If you’re struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:09.360
make simple decisions because you lack basic&nbsp;
information about the decks present, a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:15.200
lot of the depth and the skill of the EDH format&nbsp;
is stripped out, with a lot of luck replacing it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.760
This also ties in with the information density&nbsp;
of the format—in his video about me in April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:24.480
The Trinket Mage responded to my arguments about&nbsp;
the complexity of the format with a game show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:29.440
He gave me a set of cards played with a specific&nbsp;
commander, and asked if I could tell why the given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:34.240
card was in the given deck. As an exercise to&nbsp;
devote my full attention to, this was kinda fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:39.120
but the issue here is that a real commander game&nbsp;
would have a bunch of that sort of exercise, PLUS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:44.320
threat assessing between different opponents, PLUS&nbsp;
playing one’s own deck, PLUS PLUS PLUS reading a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:48.720
bunch of new cards if you’re not somebody who’s&nbsp;
already deeply embedded in the commander format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:53.360
In the context of all that extra stuff, having&nbsp;
the slightest leg up by knowing a couple key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:58.400
elements of opposing decks can really help in&nbsp;
not screwing up critical threat assessments.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:02.080
All of this brings me back to the people saying&nbsp;
“What, you want me to spill my deck’s every secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:07.280
for you?” If I’m to treat this as an entirely&nbsp;
good faith question, I would respond like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:11.680
“Hi people! No, you don’t need to do that.&nbsp;
Here’s a flowchart!” Is the part of your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:16.400
you’re thinking about a powerful wincon for you?&nbsp;
If yes, maybe consider disclosing that. If not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:19.680
is the part of your deck you’re thinking about&nbsp;
something you’d describe as your core gameplan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.000
in a way that’d be helpful to decisionmaking&nbsp;
for somebody playing against your deck? If yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:29.280
consider disclosing that. If not, you’re good!&nbsp;
We’re talking about a 10 second or less pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:32.720
you obviously don’t need to spill the beans&nbsp;
about a cool side synergy chilling deep in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:36.800
your 99. But you shouldn’t need to hide&nbsp;
the main gameplan of your deck or conceal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:42.080
the most powerful wincon. To put it bluntly, if&nbsp;
giving somebody a synopsis of what your deck does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:46.240
ruins your experience playing it, it wasn’t&nbsp;
all that interesting a deck to begin with.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:51.040
In all of this, I’ve also been dancing around&nbsp;
a final group of players: the people who think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:56.160
deception is a critical part of the commander&nbsp;
format. This is not generally a group of players I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:00.560
have a high opinion of, though to be fair some of&nbsp;
it might be a sort of trauma response to playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:05.200
with assholes. Some people in the reddit thread&nbsp;
that inspired this video mentioned concealing what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:09.520
their deck is pre-game so opponents don’t&nbsp;
try to switch to a deck that counters it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:13.040
which would be a fair consideration if you&nbsp;
accept that people treating you like shit is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:18.000
something you’re okay with in a commander game.&nbsp;
But it shouldn’t be—commander is a social format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:22.960
and if other people take pregame communication&nbsp;
as an opportunity to be an antisocial bully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:27.520
that’s a problem with them, not a problem with&nbsp;
communication as a concept. There’s also another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:32.400
group of players who specifically try to trick&nbsp;
their opponents as a strategic play, trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:37.760
sneak through wins specifically taking advantage&nbsp;
of opponents lacking knowledge of their deck. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.960
is admittedly a spectrum—there are lesser versions&nbsp;
of this like sandbagging plays and playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:47.120
small bean with a lategame centered deck, and&nbsp;
while I personally don’t really care for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:51.040
sort of table engagement I understand that it’s&nbsp;
naturally going to be a part of a multiplayer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:57.120
social format. But players doing the more serious&nbsp;
versions of this—downplaying an obviously busted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:00.960
board to somebody who doesn’t know any better,&nbsp;
pretending that a powerful centerpiece card is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.880
some normal filler, and otherwise skating&nbsp;
by on their opponents lack of experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:09.760
knowledge, or focus—this behavior is toxic to&nbsp;
any sort of casual format, and if you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:14.320
do this to win my natural assumption is that&nbsp;
you’re not very good at Magic: the Gathering.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.640
[Ian Narrating]

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.960
This video has been kind of meandering, and&nbsp;
has involved various different arguments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:26.640
favor of open communication, as well as&nbsp;
various arguments against the opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:31.920
sort of table behavior. Despite this, I’m less&nbsp;
singleminded about this than you might think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:37.200
and there are some pretty reasonable arguments for&nbsp;
less table disclosure that I’ve heard in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:41.920
One of these is that treating less experienced&nbsp;
players as full-fledged commander players, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:46.640
well as not trying to baby them with lots of extra&nbsp;
treatment, is both more respectful and a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:51.280
learning environment for a lot of players. This&nbsp;
is an idea I’m sympathetic to—different players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:55.360
do well in different environments, and a situation&nbsp;
where everybody else is playing with the kiddie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.360 --> 00:20:00.160
gloves on might not necessarily be the ideal one&nbsp;
for learning the format, depending on the player.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:05.280
Although, I think this reasoning can work the&nbsp;
other way as well—when I play a very impactful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:10.560
card, I’ll generally emphasize it to the table,&nbsp;
oftentimes saying something like “I attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:16.160
cast Captivating Crew.” To me, this is saying to&nbsp;
my opponents “I’m doing something that, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:20.320
you all as sensible players who know my gameplan&nbsp;
might want to stop, and I’m respectfully giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:25.520
you the opportunity to do so.” Same reasoning of&nbsp;
respecting my opponents’ capabilities of players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:30.240
but in this instance it leads to more table&nbsp;
communication rather than less. Regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.160
where you go with this idea, it’s important to&nbsp;
remember different players learn better under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.920
different circumstances, and we should never&nbsp;
assume we have the one-size-fits-all solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:41.920
to teaching, playing with, and showing&nbsp;
respect to less experienced players.

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:47.120
A related argument that I find compelling is that&nbsp;
over-emphasizing rule 0 as a part of the game can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:51.920
encourage a mindset of fear and avoidance in&nbsp;
newer players rather than the curiosity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:56.800
openness to experience that arguably make for a&nbsp;
better experience in commander. If players are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:01.520
immediately told to watch out for stax, to avoid&nbsp;
mana denial, to fear infect, to not play mill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:06.080
to disrespect discard, and to ask for no goad&nbsp;
in their games, suddenly you end up with a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:11.760
of players who exist in echo chambers and avoid&nbsp;
experiences they find uncomfortable. The Trinket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:16.880
Mage has discussed how newer players in his games&nbsp;
have had relatively neutral reactions to Humility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:21.760
of all cards, seeming curious about this card&nbsp;
they’d never seen before more than anything else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:25.760
While I don’t think I’d try this with one of&nbsp;
the most oppressive stax pieces in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:30.240
the broader point is an interesting one—players&nbsp;
are subtly influenced by the ways other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:35.040
react to cards, and when they haven’t been taught&nbsp;
to hate and fear a card, they’ll be more engaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:40.240
and more prepared to problem solve when playing&nbsp;
against it. This is also part of why I care more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:44.880
about the establishing expectations part of a&nbsp;
pregame conversation than I care about the rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:50.080
0 part. It’s valid for players to not want to play&nbsp;
against a particular thing, but I’d rather play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:54.640
in a landscape where the list of hard no’s for&nbsp;
players is smaller, and an alternative to keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:59.600
something like stax or combos out of a game&nbsp;
altogether is disclosing those things up front and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:03.440
allowing players the time to prepare themselves&nbsp;
for those things rather than getting blindsided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:07.920
later. As general practices, your opponents&nbsp;
should know about the most threatening parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:11.680
of your deck because that’s the part they might&nbsp;
want to strategize against. Your opponents should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:16.080
know the strongest wincon in your deck not just so&nbsp;
they can decide if they want to play against it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:20.880
but also so they can decide how they should&nbsp;
be playing against you once the game starts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:24.960
These suggestions might feel frustrating for&nbsp;
the people used to grabbing at every strand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:29.040
of advantage and playing close to the chest for&nbsp;
a better shot at winning, but I can promise you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:33.120
they’ll make for better commander games and a&nbsp;
healthier commander ecosystem in the long term.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:37.520
The final takeaway I’d want to impart here is&nbsp;
simply that the EDH format being accessible or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:43.040
good for new players or for players who play less&nbsp;
often or so on—none of these considerations are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:48.800
binaries. I will happily admit that much of the&nbsp;
EDH format’s information density is inherent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:53.920
its basic construction. The number of players,&nbsp;
the deck size, the higher life totals—all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:59.040
these things are factors in producing overwhelming&nbsp;
games for players. The lazy response to noticing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:04.720
this is to throw in the towel before you’ve even&nbsp;
begun. EDH is complicated, so it’s pointless to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:08.800
take any steps to make it more approachable or&nbsp;
more enjoyable for a wider range of players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:13.440
a coward might say. The issue with this sort of&nbsp;
statement is that the commander format, along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:19.600
with most other things in life, is not a binary.&nbsp;
It can always be modulated one way or another—even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:23.120
if commander’s inherent features mean that&nbsp;
some number of players will struggle with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:27.760
information density, there are still things we as&nbsp;
players can do to decrease the number of players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:32.320
who feel overwhelmed or fatigued, or make the&nbsp;
experience of commander more enjoyable even for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:37.040
the players who aren’t keeping up perfectly well.&nbsp;
Solutions should be viewed not based on whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:42.320
they fix all problems, but rather whether&nbsp;
they solve more problems than they create.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:46.640
There are undoubtedly some solutions that don’t&nbsp;
pass that test. Pushing players to run simpler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:50.480
and more conventional decks. Pressuring new&nbsp;
players to play a different format instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:54.720
of commander. Asking Wizards of the Coast to&nbsp;
get heavily involved in fixing commander’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:58.800
information density problem. These are all&nbsp;
things that you could argue might help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:03.520
but they leave open the potential for all sorts&nbsp;
of negative side effects. What isn’t loaded with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:08.080
potential negative side effects is talking&nbsp;
to your opponents. You don’t need to share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:12.400
your deck’s life story or list out every card&nbsp;
or any other piece of hyperbole thrown out by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:17.200
some denizen of the internet. My video a year&nbsp;
ago spent a while going over suggestions so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:21.760
I’ll keep it brief and simple here. Treat your&nbsp;
opponents like people you’re playing a game with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:26.000
and give them the information they need to&nbsp;
engage with your deck on a basic level. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:29.760
you’re worried that 10 seconds of talking about&nbsp;
your deck is going to ruin its fun surprises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:33.760
that’s probably a good indicator that the&nbsp;
surprises aren’t actually fun and that all you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:38.240
have on your hands is a gimmick that you and/or&nbsp;
everyone you know will be sick of in approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:43.360
27 seconds. Finally, it’s okay if you enjoy&nbsp;
any of the versions of information-limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:47.120
commander I’ve been complaining about in&nbsp;
this video—I even see the appeal of some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:51.520
aspects of that style. At the end of the day,&nbsp;
the biggest takeaway here should simply be to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:55.600
keep audience in mind—what’s fun for you isn’t&nbsp;
inherently going to be fun for other people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:59.840
what you can handle cognitively isn’t inherently&nbsp;
going to be handled as well by other people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:04.880
and what works for one table might not work for&nbsp;
all tables. Casual EDH is a format inherently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:10.320
built on compromise and collaboration, and leaning&nbsp;
into that will only lead to having more fun.

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:14.240
Thanks for watching, and thanks to Archidekt&nbsp;
for supporting the channel. I recently did a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:18.480
deck review on my second channel, and the flexible&nbsp;
UI of Archidekt was so helpful for both helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:23.040
me analyze decks and helping me display their&nbsp;
different elements. A link to all of my decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:28.000
is in the description. Also, shout out to all of&nbsp;
these people for being cool creators and lending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:33.280
their vocal talents in this video. A link to each&nbsp;
of their channels is in the description. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:36.160
thanks to my Patrons and YouTube members&nbsp;
for their ongoing support of everything I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:41.440
do here. I have some extra thoughts about how&nbsp;
brackets interact with rule 0 that didn’t make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:44.560
sense to put anywhere in this video, so&nbsp;
I’ve tossed those in a three-minute video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:48.400
for supporter. I wanted to get this bad boy out&nbsp;
the door so that bit’s not quite finished yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:52.240
but keep your eyes peeled for that, it’ll&nbsp;
be out in the next couple days. Saloob out.

